00:01:43.180 --> 00:01:47.579
Speaker 1: Status epilepticus remains one of the most time-critical emergencies we face.

00:01:47.860 --> 00:01:54.659
Speaker 1: Prolonged seizures can lead to severe acidosis, causing cardiovascular collapse and CNS damage.

00:01:55.140 --> 00:01:59.140
Speaker 1: Time-based definitions have shortened in the last decade or so.

00:01:59.439 --> 00:02:06.159
Speaker 1: Mortality remains high, and our seizure, cessation, airway, and sedation strategies are evolving.

00:02:06.399 --> 00:02:08.399
Speaker 1: So it's time for an update.

00:02:09.020 --> 00:02:16.860
Speaker 1: If you haven't listened to our Big Main episode podcast on Status Epilepticus already, episode number 133, I encourage you to do so.

00:02:17.019 --> 00:02:18.620
Speaker 1: It's one of my personal faves.

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:32.480
Speaker 1: Now, there are lots of controversies when it comes to how best to manage status epilepticus, and as we discussed in this update, we'll try to make clear what is evidence-based and not so controversial, and what is opinion-based and controversial.

00:02:32.540 --> 00:02:43.260
Speaker 1: So it's a pleasure and honor, as always, to have back on the show and in the EMC studios my friend, master educator, EM speaker, and EM Doc Coach.

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:51.560
Speaker 1: who's worked both as an intensivist and EM doc for about the same number of years as I've been working as an EM doc, Dr.

00:02:51.640 --> 00:02:52.680
Speaker 1: Sarah Gray.

00:02:52.920 --> 00:02:56.040
Speaker 1: Great to have you back at EMC Studios again, Sarah.

00:02:56.360 --> 00:02:57.959
Speaker 3: Thanks so much for having me.

00:02:58.379 --> 00:03:09.900
Speaker 1: So before we get into the status epilepticus management algorithm and chat about specific decision points, I just want to hit on the sort of game changers that are most likely to determine outcomes.

00:03:10.220 --> 00:03:20.700
Speaker 1: So what would you say are the most important moves or aspects of management that are likely to decrease the incredibly high mortality and disability that can result from status epilepticus?

00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:27.660
Speaker 1: You know, there's there's time to first benzo, there's the correct dosing of benzos, there's aspiration risk, there's like so many aspects.

00:03:27.819 --> 00:03:35.260
Speaker 1: But I just want to try and focus our listeners on the most important life-saving things that we can do in status epilepticus.

00:03:35.799 --> 00:03:39.560
Speaker 3: Okay, and I would say, Anton, I probably think about three things.

00:03:39.799 --> 00:03:42.040
Speaker 3: Number one, get a glucose, right?

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:44.360
Speaker 3: It's so easy, but we don't want to overlook it.

00:03:44.599 --> 00:03:45.480
Speaker 3: Number two.

00:03:46.140 --> 00:03:49.019
Speaker 3: Time to your first benzodose matters.

00:03:49.180 --> 00:03:52.939
Speaker 3: So the quicker you give it, the lower mortality in status epilepticus.

00:03:53.019 --> 00:03:57.019
Speaker 3: So you want to think about that as a serious quality marker here.

00:03:57.240 --> 00:04:07.080
Speaker 3: And number three, the duration of the seizure makes a huge difference in terms of outcomes, in terms of mortality, but also morbidity from seizure.

00:04:07.260 --> 00:04:12.460
Speaker 3: So turning off status as fast as possible has a huge mortality impact.

00:04:13.020 --> 00:04:13.340
Speaker 1: All right.

00:04:13.580 --> 00:04:19.740
Speaker 1: So ABCDEFG and the D-E-F-G stands for don't ever forget glucose.

00:04:19.940 --> 00:04:23.940
Speaker 1: Which of course I have forgotten quite a few times over the years.

00:04:24.580 --> 00:04:25.780
Speaker 3: Right, we all have.

00:04:25.940 --> 00:04:28.980
Speaker 3: Uh and hopefully somebody in the room prompts you or reminds you.

00:04:29.380 --> 00:04:29.540
Speaker 3: But

00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:35.440
Speaker 3: But yeah, uh I know it's such an easy point, but it is worth emphasizing because we're all human and we forget sometimes.

00:04:35.840 --> 00:04:36.000
Speaker 1: Great.

00:04:36.080 --> 00:04:38.960
Speaker 1: So those three things we should really be concentrating on.

00:04:39.160 --> 00:04:41.240
Speaker 1: And we'll come back to in our discussion.

00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:49.080
Speaker 1: So glucose, time to first benzo, and really that the faster we get this thing done, the better the outcome is going to be.

00:04:49.400 --> 00:04:49.800
Speaker 3: Exactly.

00:04:50.040 --> 00:04:50.360
Speaker 1: Okay.

00:04:50.639 --> 00:04:55.440
Speaker 1: So let's actually zero in on minimizing time to first benzo.

00:04:55.680 --> 00:04:58.720
Speaker 1: In the main episode we did it on Status Epilepticus

00:04:59.039 --> 00:05:08.000
Speaker 1: We argued that time to first benzo, as you astutely said, is so important that it actually is more important than your choice of benzo.

00:05:08.720 --> 00:05:11.520
Speaker 1: There's a bit of controversy out there as to the choice of benzo.

00:05:11.600 --> 00:05:14.880
Speaker 1: You know, there's larzepam, there's diazepam, there's medazolam.

00:05:15.280 --> 00:05:18.960
Speaker 1: There's some benefits of midazolam that has a it's short acting.

00:05:19.420 --> 00:05:31.020
Speaker 1: that it has predictable pharmacokinetics, it has high CNS penetration, but the only problem with that is that medazolam is going to turn off pretty quickly, and then if you don't have it going as a chunky infusion

00:05:31.160 --> 00:05:34.440
Speaker 1: Uh then the patient might re-sease again and they're still in status.

00:05:35.240 --> 00:05:42.760
Speaker 1: Given that we understand that time to benzo is really important and we want that as sure as possible, we do need to choose a benzo.

00:05:42.840 --> 00:05:45.480
Speaker 1: What's your benzo of choice and why?

00:05:45.880 --> 00:05:50.680
Speaker 3: So if I have an IV, I go with Lirazepam as my benzo of choice.

00:05:50.919 --> 00:05:53.720
Speaker 3: I like this because the onset is quick

00:05:53.639 --> 00:05:57.000
Speaker 3: And because it sticks around effectively in your CNS.

00:05:57.240 --> 00:06:01.880
Speaker 3: Because it's low in lipophilicity, you're going to have longer CNS effects

00:06:01.700 --> 00:06:04.500
Speaker 3: So that's my first choice if I have an IV.

00:06:04.740 --> 00:06:09.300
Speaker 3: If I don't have an IV available to me, I go with I am Medazalam.

00:06:09.560 --> 00:06:11.160
Speaker 3: Which is also a very reasonable choice.

00:06:11.400 --> 00:06:18.440
Speaker 3: Onset is fast, but as you mentioned, it's going to wear off a little quicker, so you want to be very thoughtful about repeating dosing if you need.

00:06:18.760 --> 00:06:19.000
Speaker 3: All right.

00:06:19.160 --> 00:06:21.240
Speaker 1: So Ivy Lurazepam is your go-to.

00:06:21.480 --> 00:06:26.520
Speaker 1: And I just want to talk about the dosing of that because the guidelines say 0.

00:06:26.680 --> 00:06:30.920
Speaker 1: 1 milligrams per kilogram to a maximum of four milligrams.

00:06:31.320 --> 00:06:43.640
Speaker 1: But let's say you have someone who's a known alcohol use disorder patient who has had multiple withdrawal seizures in the past and have required massive doses of benzodiazepines.

00:06:43.680 --> 00:06:47.760
Speaker 1: Four milligrams of Lurazepam is gonna do nothing for their status epilepticus.

00:06:47.920 --> 00:06:49.920
Speaker 1: That's just one extreme example.

00:06:50.160 --> 00:06:51.680
Speaker 1: But I have heard that

00:06:52.139 --> 00:07:01.259
Speaker 1: the guideline recommended maximum dose of four milligrams of Lirazepam is probably too low and that we should be doing more like eight milligrams.

00:07:01.560 --> 00:07:05.960
Speaker 1: for the average size adult as your first line status epilepticus benzo.

00:07:06.280 --> 00:07:08.759
Speaker 1: What's your opinion on on the dosing of the Razopam?

00:07:09.260 --> 00:07:14.140
Speaker 3: So certainly if somebody has known alcohol use disorder, I am willing to go higher.

00:07:11.000 --> 00:07:11.240
Speaker 3: For

00:07:14.380 --> 00:07:18.780
Speaker 3: Some experts will recommend that we double our normal doses.

00:07:19.340 --> 00:07:24.940
Speaker 3: If I was going to do that, I do want to be very sure that this patient has ethanol withdrawal.

00:07:25.100 --> 00:07:27.500
Speaker 3: Like I want to be sure of my diagnosis.

00:07:27.660 --> 00:07:35.980
Speaker 3: before I truly snow them, but there are definitely patients where they are well known to me, the diagnosis is clear, and I will double those doses.

00:07:36.140 --> 00:07:40.620
Speaker 3: So rather than giving them an initial four milligrams of Lorazepam, I will give them eight milligrams.

00:07:40.820 --> 00:07:50.580
Speaker 3: If you're not comfortable doubling the dose up front and giving that 8 milligram bolus, then maybe think about giving 4 milligrams each time, but every two to three minutes

00:07:50.639 --> 00:07:54.720
Speaker 3: repeating doses more frequently so you get them to an adequate overall dose.

00:07:55.120 --> 00:07:55.280
Speaker 1: Great.

00:07:55.360 --> 00:08:01.600
Speaker 1: So that was actually my next question is you've done your, let's say, four milligrams of Lirazepam, when are you going to repeat it?

00:08:01.740 --> 00:08:07.740
Speaker 1: So do you usually repeat every two, three minutes or there's been recommendations for every four minutes?

00:08:08.060 --> 00:08:11.260
Speaker 3: Yeah, I'm around the two to three minute mark, I would say.

00:08:11.420 --> 00:08:13.100
Speaker 3: I do have to start a watch for that.

00:08:13.180 --> 00:08:15.820
Speaker 3: So seizures are something I start a timer for.

00:08:15.660 --> 00:08:20.460
Speaker 3: Because otherwise my mental clock thinks three minutes have gone by and really it's been about forty-five seconds.

00:08:20.700 --> 00:08:21.660
Speaker 3: Uh so

00:08:23.800 --> 00:08:32.919
Speaker 3: Yeah, my my perception of time in these situations uh gets a bit compressed, so I definitely start a clock, and then yeah, I'd say every three minutes or so I'm redosing benzodiazepines.

00:08:33.140 --> 00:08:33.380
Speaker 1: Okay.

00:08:33.620 --> 00:08:40.820
Speaker 1: The other patient population I'm thinking of in terms of maybe going for a higher dose of Lurazepem is is the patient with a high BMI.

00:08:41.240 --> 00:08:48.120
Speaker 3: Yes, and so it certainly can make a difference, especially if you're using something quite lipophilic.

00:08:48.280 --> 00:08:52.280
Speaker 3: On the upside, lorazipam is fairly low in lipophilicity.

00:08:52.520 --> 00:09:00.520
Speaker 3: So it's not gonna have as much of an impact based on your patient's BMI, but it could still be the case, you know, you might need a little more.

00:09:00.600 --> 00:09:02.040
Speaker 3: So I I think that's worth

00:09:02.259 --> 00:09:06.019
Speaker 3: You know, putting into your mental algorithm and having a little flexibility there.

00:09:06.259 --> 00:09:09.779
Speaker 3: But I don't have a specific rule for patients who are obese.

00:09:10.320 --> 00:09:16.080
Speaker 1: It's a good point about the pharmacokinetics of Lirazepam that it probably won't make much of a difference in the high BMI patient.

00:09:16.240 --> 00:09:17.680
Speaker 1: With diazepam it would.

00:09:18.000 --> 00:09:18.240
Speaker 3: Exactly.

00:09:18.560 --> 00:09:18.720
Speaker 1: Yeah.

00:09:18.880 --> 00:09:19.040
Speaker 1: Okay.

00:09:19.200 --> 00:09:20.480
Speaker 1: And how about MEDAZ?

00:09:20.940 --> 00:09:23.100
Speaker 3: Yeah, so it will also have an impact on Madaz.

00:09:23.260 --> 00:09:25.580
Speaker 3: I think like it will have some impact on all of them.

00:09:25.740 --> 00:09:27.980
Speaker 3: They all have a degree of fat distribution here.

00:09:28.660 --> 00:09:37.460
Speaker 3: And so, you know, I think the the important takeaway is to titrate your medication to the patient you see in front of you.

00:09:37.640 --> 00:09:45.320
Speaker 3: Putting BMI into that, putting their ethanol or benzouse history, putting their age, their blood pressure, all of those things go into the mix.

00:09:45.360 --> 00:09:46.400
Speaker 1: Fair enough.

00:09:52.320 --> 00:09:57.120
Speaker 1: And just a quick word from one of our sponsors, the Rural Ultrasound Fellowship.

00:09:57.339 --> 00:10:01.660
Speaker 1: No matter where you practice, there are moments when decisions can't wait.

00:10:01.819 --> 00:10:07.740
Speaker 1: Maybe imaging isn't available or the system is too busy and you still need clear answers right now.

00:10:08.060 --> 00:10:13.340
Speaker 1: That's where a POCUS Fellowship makes all the difference and gives you the confidence at the bedside.

00:10:13.500 --> 00:10:18.700
Speaker 1: It helps you decide that you can manage locally and when it's time to escalate care.

