Speaker 1: The Rural Ultrasound Fellowship is a one-year fully online POCUS training program you can complete while working full-time, taught by international faculty.

00:10:28.460 --> 00:10:30.780
Speaker 1: Learn more at ruralultrasound.

00:10:31.100 --> 00:10:31.420
Speaker 1: com.

00:10:31.820 --> 00:10:33.900
Speaker 1: built for real-world decisions.

00:10:34.380 --> 00:10:43.260
Speaker 1: Okay, so so far we've talked about time to benzo is key and that your go-to benzo of choice, yours personally is Larazopam.

00:10:43.340 --> 00:10:45.740
Speaker 1: Some people use Ivy Medazolam.

00:10:46.120 --> 00:10:49.800
Speaker 1: And I've heard 5 milligrams up front of IV Medazolem.

00:10:49.959 --> 00:10:53.160
Speaker 1: I've also heard 10 milligrams up front of IV Medazolem

00:10:53.660 --> 00:10:55.500
Speaker 1: It does have great pharmacokinetics.

00:10:55.580 --> 00:10:58.460
Speaker 1: It's a fast onset, so it's a reasonable first choice.

00:10:58.620 --> 00:11:06.380
Speaker 1: The problem with it again is that it turns off very quickly, and if you're not totally on top of repeated doses and getting an infusion going.

00:11:06.640 --> 00:11:08.640
Speaker 1: that they might reseize again.

00:11:08.880 --> 00:11:11.440
Speaker 1: I want to talk a little bit more about specific populations.

00:11:11.520 --> 00:11:14.720
Speaker 1: We've talked a little bit about patients with alcohol use disorder.

00:11:15.060 --> 00:11:21.060
Speaker 1: Because when we're treating these patients, it's not always going to be just benzos and then going through our algorithm.

00:11:21.220 --> 00:11:26.660
Speaker 1: We need to think about those patient populations where our first line medication is actually going to be different than a benzo.

00:11:26.759 --> 00:11:34.920
Speaker 1: So can you just kind of run through for us, you know, there's the hyponatremia patient, there's the eclamptic patient, there's various TOX patients.

00:11:35.259 --> 00:11:40.220
Speaker 1: Can you just run through for us how your algorithm is going to change based on those different patient populations?

00:11:40.620 --> 00:11:41.420
Speaker 3: Yeah, absolutely.

00:11:41.579 --> 00:11:43.420
Speaker 3: Maybe let's tackle that one at a time.

00:11:43.640 --> 00:11:51.400
Speaker 3: If you have the hyponotremic plus seizing patient, they're going to follow into a different algorithm.

00:11:51.300 --> 00:11:55.459
Speaker 3: And I guess this is predicated on you knowing their sodium level.

00:11:55.620 --> 00:11:59.220
Speaker 3: Because if the patient comes in cold, you may not know, you might start with

00:11:59.360 --> 00:12:00.720
Speaker 3: benzos up front.

00:12:00.960 --> 00:12:08.320
Speaker 3: So I guess it is a good reminder that seizing patients or patients in status epilepticus certainly you want to send electrolytes.

00:12:08.459 --> 00:12:11.980
Speaker 3: You want to know what their sodium is because it's going to change your management.

00:12:12.140 --> 00:12:18.380
Speaker 3: So for those patients, I'll send a VBG with electrolytes on it because that's what my lab can process the fastest.

00:12:18.580 --> 00:12:20.100
Speaker 3: So I get those results back quicker.

00:12:20.420 --> 00:12:21.620
Speaker 1: Yeah, that's a great pearl.

00:12:21.780 --> 00:12:23.460
Speaker 1: So uh some places you can't do that.

00:12:23.620 --> 00:12:33.140
Speaker 1: I'd suggest to listeners to advocate for getting electrolytes off your VBG, making sure your lab can do that, because that can save lives for sure.

00:12:32.959 --> 00:12:37.519
Speaker 1: Because if you're just treating the hyponatremic patient just with benzos, you're not going to get very far.

00:12:37.680 --> 00:12:42.240
Speaker 1: And it's a pretty quick fix with with hypertonic saline, sodium bicarb, et cetera.

00:12:42.360 --> 00:12:52.200
Speaker 3: Yeah, I mean, and I've had that case where the patient comes in, we think they're in status, we're into our second, third, fourth line medications and nothing's turning it off.

00:12:52.360 --> 00:12:55.640
Speaker 3: And then the lab calls and tells you the sodium's a hundred and eight.

00:12:55.720 --> 00:13:02.120
Speaker 3: Uh and everything screeches to a halt as you start getting your 3% and and your treatment goes in a different direction.

00:13:02.440 --> 00:13:06.600
Speaker 3: So sending it is is a key move because it definitely changes practice.

00:13:06.920 --> 00:13:07.000
Speaker 1: Yeah.

00:13:07.080 --> 00:13:08.360
Speaker 1: So that's another great point.

00:13:08.600 --> 00:13:09.160
Speaker 1: Just

00:13:08.900 --> 00:13:23.220
Speaker 1: When your benzo is not working, this is exactly when you gotta start thinking about the other causes, like hyponeitremia, and we'll get to the other ones, but before we do get to the other ones, what are you gonna give for severe hyponia in a patient who's actively in status?

00:13:23.800 --> 00:13:27.080
Speaker 3: So my preference is to give hypertonic saline.

00:13:27.240 --> 00:13:35.000
Speaker 3: At my place we stalk 3% saline, and I'll typically give that in aliquots of about 100 cc's.

00:13:35.160 --> 00:13:36.200
Speaker 3: You give it IV.

00:13:36.420 --> 00:13:38.180
Speaker 3: You can give it over a minute or so.

00:13:38.420 --> 00:13:44.740
Speaker 3: For places that don't stock hypertonic saline, your alternate is to give an amp of sodium bicarb

00:13:45.040 --> 00:13:52.880
Speaker 3: which everybody has 'cause it's stalked on crash carts, and we think of that as bicarb, but you also want to think of it as a significant sodium load.

00:13:52.959 --> 00:13:56.880
Speaker 3: So there's fifty mil equivalents of sodium in each one of those syringes.

00:13:56.800 --> 00:14:04.959
Speaker 3: And so you can also think about giving repeated doses of that sodium bicarb until their neurologic status improves.

00:14:05.339 --> 00:14:06.220
Speaker 1: All right, fantastic.

00:14:06.380 --> 00:14:08.060
Speaker 1: So that's hyponatremia.

00:14:08.139 --> 00:14:14.139
Speaker 1: There's preeclampsia, there's hypertensive encephalopathy, there's all the sodium blockade talks drugs.

00:14:14.339 --> 00:14:17.220
Speaker 1: Can you give us uh any tips or tricks about those ones?

00:14:17.699 --> 00:14:18.180
Speaker 3: Absolutely.

00:14:18.339 --> 00:14:18.660
Speaker 3: Yeah.

00:14:18.819 --> 00:14:22.660
Speaker 3: So when your patient's eclamptic, you're going to go in a different direction, right?

00:14:22.740 --> 00:14:25.220
Speaker 3: Your fix there is magnesium.

00:14:25.300 --> 00:14:33.380
Speaker 3: So typically I'll give them a bolus of magnesium between four to six grams, and you can put that in over 15 minutes or so.

00:14:33.300 --> 00:14:40.180
Speaker 3: You want to watch for hypotension because it does give you a bit of vasodilation and you might need to support their blood pressure with some norepi.

00:14:40.580 --> 00:14:45.860
Speaker 3: And once you've given the bolus, they then get an infusion that you can titrate to how they're doing

00:14:45.780 --> 00:14:47.620
Speaker 3: So that's your eclamtic person.

00:14:47.700 --> 00:14:53.620
Speaker 3: You don't want to forget about magnesium and obviously getting your OB-Gyne service involved in those people.

00:14:53.860 --> 00:14:59.220
Speaker 3: For the hypertensive patient, controlling the blood pressure is your core move there.

00:14:59.380 --> 00:15:01.300
Speaker 3: I use Libetolol a lot for that.

00:15:01.460 --> 00:15:04.980
Speaker 3: You can give boluses, say, of 20 milligrams at a time.

00:15:04.980 --> 00:15:11.620
Speaker 3: until you get the blood pressure down into the range you're looking for, and you're also clinically, of course, looking for your patient to stop seizing.

00:15:11.940 --> 00:15:16.500
Speaker 3: Nicardipine is also a great option there, depending on where you practice.

00:15:16.920 --> 00:15:24.519
Speaker 3: So you have choices and you want to be clear on what your department stocks in advance so you know what you're going to use in that circumstance.

00:15:24.380 --> 00:15:28.140
Speaker 1: So that's the differential we should be thinking through when our benzos aren't working.

00:15:28.380 --> 00:15:38.940
Speaker 1: Hopefully we know that they're, you know, twenty eight weeks pregnant and they come in with a high blood pressure and they're seizing and that's gonna be obvious, but sometimes it's not so obvious.

00:15:39.220 --> 00:15:46.260
Speaker 3: I guess I'm making a mental note that I should at least be examining the abdomen and if I'm having any uncertainty, sticking a probe on.

00:15:46.500 --> 00:15:46.820
Speaker 1: Hmm.

00:15:47.160 --> 00:15:55.000
Speaker 1: And certainly, you know, if they're postpartum, it's up to, I believe, three or four weeks that they can still have a clampsia after.

00:15:55.160 --> 00:15:55.800
Speaker 1: Um and

00:15:56.160 --> 00:15:56.960
Speaker 1: That's a bit trickier.

00:15:57.040 --> 00:15:59.040
Speaker 1: That you gotta find on on history.

00:15:59.280 --> 00:16:07.840
Speaker 1: So that's where if the patient comes in with altered level of awareness, you need to get on the phone or to the family or to someone to

00:16:08.220 --> 00:16:10.700
Speaker 1: find out if they've just delivered a baby or not.

00:16:10.940 --> 00:16:12.860
Speaker 3: Yeah, you're gonna need some collateral there.

00:16:13.740 --> 00:16:16.700
Speaker 1: We've been talking a lot about the timing of benzos

00:16:17.060 --> 00:16:24.260
Speaker 1: That you want to give them as fast as possible, that you want to give your second dose after about two minutes if the seizure is continuing.

00:16:24.660 --> 00:16:28.260
Speaker 1: That brings up the definition of status epilepticus.

00:16:28.520 --> 00:16:31.960
Speaker 1: Which in the old days was a seizure over thirty minutes.

00:16:32.040 --> 00:16:35.880
Speaker 1: And now for about a decade now it's been any seizure over five minutes, right?

00:16:36.200 --> 00:16:39.080
Speaker 1: Or a seizure that has recurred within five minutes.

00:16:39.480 --> 00:16:41.240
Speaker 3: Where they don't come back to baseline in the middle.

00:16:41.320 --> 00:16:41.560
Speaker 3: Yep.

00:16:41.880 --> 00:16:42.520
Speaker 1: Right.

00:16:42.860 --> 00:16:43.980
Speaker 3: And that's quick, right?

00:16:44.300 --> 00:16:45.420
Speaker 1: Yeah, that's really fast.

00:16:45.660 --> 00:16:53.260
Speaker 1: So the question is, you know, we see quite a few patients with seizures in the emergency department who who start their seizure in the emergency department.

00:16:53.700 --> 00:17:02.980
Speaker 1: And I always am unsure whether to go for like the big guns, you know, am I gonna give five or ten of Ivy Medazam or four to eight?

00:17:03.160 --> 00:17:11.640
Speaker 1: milligrams of larazipam up front for the patient who's been seizing for one minute because I don't know if they're going to go into status epilepticus.

00:17:11.720 --> 00:17:13.800
Speaker 1: I don't know if it's going to last for five minutes or more.

00:17:14.319 --> 00:17:20.160
Speaker 1: the vast majority of seizures resolve within five minutes, even by themselves.

00:17:20.720 --> 00:17:24.079
Speaker 1: So I'm always unsure like how aggressive to be

00:17:24.160 --> 00:17:26.400
Speaker 1: after it's one minute or two minutes of seizure.

00:17:26.640 --> 00:17:30.320
Speaker 1: Do you have an approach to how fast to give that first benzo?

00:17:30.480 --> 00:17:35.760
Speaker 1: And before they fit the definition for status epilepticus, how aggressive to be in your dosing?

00:17:36.580 --> 00:17:45.539
Speaker 3: Yes, so I'll tell you my approach, Anton, and this is my opinion, because you will hear experts who have an alternate opinion.

00:17:45.500 --> 00:17:53.180
Speaker 3: Some people don't treat in those first five minutes because so many patients will spontaneously stop seizing on their own.

00:17:53.440 --> 00:18:03.760
Speaker 3: I am always worried that if this patient continues on to status, that delaying that first dose actually worsens outcomes and

00:17:55.100 --> 00:17:55.260
Speaker 3: S

00:18:03.900 --> 00:18:05.980
Speaker 3: Like those patients have a real morbidity.

00:18:06.060 --> 00:18:13.820
Speaker 3: If you have status epilepticus, 30% of those people end up with a moderate to severe neurodeficit from that episode.

00:18:13.980 --> 00:18:14.620
Speaker 3: Like that's

00:18:15.120 --> 00:18:15.760
Speaker 3: Huge.

00:18:16.320 --> 00:18:19.680
Speaker 3: And so I want to err on the side of treating them.

00:18:20.080 --> 00:18:21.920
Speaker 1: My understanding is it's like a snowball effect.

00:18:22.000 --> 00:18:24.960
Speaker 1: Like the longer the seizure goes on, the harder it is to get them out of it.

00:18:24.960 --> 00:18:25.280
Speaker 3: Exactly.

00:18:25.520 --> 00:18:30.240
Speaker 1: And so, yeah, to me it kind of makes no sense just to see if someone's going to resolve their seizure.

00:18:30.720 --> 00:18:31.280
Speaker 3: Right.

00:18:31.600 --> 00:18:34.720
Speaker 3: And so for me, if somebody's seizing

00:18:34.800 --> 00:18:40.160
Speaker 3: I'm asking my nurses for Lorazepam right away, and I'm gonna give them that upfront dose.

00:18:40.320 --> 00:18:43.280
Speaker 3: Uh if their seizure stops, great, that's a win.

00:18:43.440 --> 00:18:45.840
Speaker 3: If it doesn't, we've started treatment early.

00:18:45.960 --> 00:18:55.160
Speaker 3: Either way, I don't think there's a compelling reason to just stand there and let them seize, because we know that they're burning neurons.

00:18:55.700 --> 00:18:56.020
Speaker 1: All right.

00:18:56.180 --> 00:19:01.060
Speaker 1: And in terms of the dosing, you're still going to go with the four milligrams of Lirazepam up front?

00:19:01.140 --> 00:19:04.580
Speaker 1: Because I've seen people, you know, after one minute, they'll just try two milligrams.

00:19:04.660 --> 00:19:07.460
Speaker 1: And then two minutes later, they'll try another two milligrams.

00:19:07.840 --> 00:19:12.480
Speaker 3: And the guidelines on this are not clear, so I don't think that's unreasonable.

00:19:12.720 --> 00:19:14.960
Speaker 3: You could go two milligrams first.

00:19:15.200 --> 00:19:17.600
Speaker 3: You can also base it on how quick it's been.

00:19:17.760 --> 00:19:25.679
Speaker 3: Like if you were able to get the drugs to the bedside really rapidly, patient's been seizing 60 seconds or so, I think two milligrams is reasonable.

00:19:25.860 --> 00:19:38.340
Speaker 3: If it took a little longer to get the drugs to the bedside, because sometimes the med room is just that far away, and you're closer to that three, four, five minute mark, then I'm gonna go four milligrams and treat it a bit more aggressively.

00:19:38.659 --> 00:19:40.500
Speaker 1: Yeah, and I I think realistically

00:19:40.660 --> 00:19:47.780
Speaker 1: It usually takes a good three, four minutes just to get the medication into the patient from the time that it's been recognized that they start seizing.

00:19:48.100 --> 00:19:48.580
Speaker 3: Exactly.

00:19:48.740 --> 00:19:55.300
Speaker 3: And and sometimes, truthfully, I'll ask for the drug and then while the nurses are getting it, the patient stops seizing.

00:19:55.540 --> 00:19:56.020
Speaker 3: Fine.

00:19:56.180 --> 00:20:00.420
Speaker 3: Then I have a syringe at the bedside ready to go in case they do recur.

00:20:00.820 --> 00:20:06.100
Speaker 3: Which just seems safe to me to be prepped for the next occurrence if it's gonna happen.

00:20:06.360 --> 00:20:14.279
Speaker 1: Yeah, and the other point is if I gave you four milligrams of IV diazepam right now, assuming we were in the emergency department, it's pretty safe.

00:20:14.360 --> 00:20:15.880
Speaker 1: I mean, you're in an emergency department.

00:20:16.080 --> 00:20:22.960
Speaker 1: And benzodiazepines are pretty safe drugs as long as you're there to manage the airway if there's a problem, right?

00:20:23.360 --> 00:20:23.840
Speaker 3: Exactly.

00:20:24.000 --> 00:20:24.240
Speaker 3: Right?

00:20:24.320 --> 00:20:27.520
Speaker 3: And they might have a a nice little nap for a few minutes.

00:20:27.820 --> 00:20:31.020
Speaker 3: But it's also very safe for their brain as long as you're monitoring them.

00:20:31.340 --> 00:20:31.500
Speaker 1: Yeah.

00:20:31.660 --> 00:20:33.580
Speaker 1: So there's very little downside.

00:20:33.900 --> 00:20:36.060
Speaker 1: So I think, you know, the more we talk about this

00:20:36.620 --> 00:20:45.420
Speaker 1: For me, my approach is going to be even if it's just one minute of seizure, I'm still going to go for that higher dose, at least four milligrams of Lurazepam.

00:20:45.560 --> 00:20:51.240
Speaker 1: 'Cause I can manage that easily if it's a bit of a dose that's too high for that patient for whatever reason.

00:20:51.560 --> 00:20:58.680
Speaker 1: Because even if there's only a three percent chance or a five percent chance of them developing status epilepticus, I think

00:20:58.940 --> 00:21:06.940
Speaker 1: with the high mortality and morbidity associated with status epilepticus, it's in the balance, it's it's worth giving that big dose of larzepam.

00:21:07.540 --> 00:21:09.140
Speaker 3: Yeah, I totally agree.

