Speaker 1: com slash em cases.

00:21:53.920 --> 00:21:56.800
Speaker 1: Let's talk about emergency medicine's favorite drug.

00:21:56.779 --> 00:21:57.419
Speaker 1: Ketamine.

00:21:58.220 --> 00:21:59.740
Speaker 1: Ketamine fixes everything.

00:22:00.220 --> 00:22:00.940
Speaker 3: Everything.

00:22:01.260 --> 00:22:02.700
Speaker 3: Even your sprained ankle.

00:22:02.940 --> 00:22:03.419
Speaker 1: Yeah.

00:22:03.980 --> 00:22:10.539
Speaker 1: We've maybe just hinted at what the role of ketamine is in status epilepticus in our opinion, but

00:22:10.640 --> 00:22:14.880
Speaker 1: There's been a lot of controversy around using ketamine and status epilepticus.

00:22:15.040 --> 00:22:20.080
Speaker 1: There's been some growing practice of adding ketamine one milligram per kilogram.

00:22:20.160 --> 00:22:21.840
Speaker 1: So this is a decent dose.

00:22:22.060 --> 00:22:25.260
Speaker 1: You know, in an average adult, that's 70 milligrams.

00:22:25.420 --> 00:22:32.380
Speaker 1: You know, in a high BMI patient, we're talking, you know, 100 or 120 or 125 of ketamine IV.

00:22:33.019 --> 00:22:37.419
Speaker 1: And some experts recommend giving that with your second dose of benzo.

00:22:37.740 --> 00:22:45.580
Speaker 1: So let's say we've given Lerazepam four milligrams, two minutes later, seizure hasn't stopped, you've given another four milligrams with that.

00:22:45.779 --> 00:22:49.460
Speaker 1: You give ketamine, let's say 70 milligrams.

00:22:50.019 --> 00:22:57.539
Speaker 1: And I believe this is based on one pediatric RCT called the KET-MID trial.

00:22:57.760 --> 00:23:00.720
Speaker 1: And there's been a couple of observational trials in adults.

00:23:00.799 --> 00:23:02.960
Speaker 1: I think there was a prospective analysis.

00:23:03.120 --> 00:23:04.960
Speaker 1: There's been a lot of debate on this.

00:23:05.200 --> 00:23:07.440
Speaker 1: Our good friend Justin Morgenstern.

00:23:07.940 --> 00:23:14.500
Speaker 1: had a debate with Scott Weingart on MCRIT and uh Justin's got some great posts on this on first 10 EM.

00:23:15.139 --> 00:23:21.460
Speaker 1: What's your read on the evidence signal for ketamine in status epilepticus and where does it

00:23:21.740 --> 00:23:25.900
Speaker 1: fit in in terms of like your algorithm or your real-world experience?

00:23:26.780 --> 00:23:30.700
Speaker 3: I would say my bottom line is I'm still waiting for better data.

00:23:31.040 --> 00:23:35.200
Speaker 3: So I'm not using it yet as a second line medication.

00:23:35.280 --> 00:23:37.920
Speaker 3: For me and my algorithm, it's probably third or fourth line.

00:23:38.160 --> 00:23:42.720
Speaker 3: I am really interested in this area and we want to see what signal is coming out.

00:23:42.700 --> 00:23:45.740
Speaker 3: The CET mid trial in pediatrics was positive.

00:23:45.820 --> 00:23:50.539
Speaker 3: That was 144 kids and RCT, and they showed it was beneficial there.

00:23:50.779 --> 00:23:54.940
Speaker 3: That's the only RCT that's shown positivity so far.

00:23:54.900 --> 00:23:59.220
Speaker 3: The Kesset trial is still enrolling, so it'll be great to see that data once it's available.

00:23:59.460 --> 00:24:09.220
Speaker 3: The two adult trials that I'm most familiar with are just observational cohorts where the people who happened to get ketamine in there did better.

00:24:09.260 --> 00:24:12.140
Speaker 3: Some of them didn't get ketamine in the first day.

00:24:12.380 --> 00:24:17.820
Speaker 3: Like one of them was a longer trial where they start getting it after really prolonged episodes of Status epilepticus.

00:24:18.220 --> 00:24:21.500
Speaker 3: So I think it's not in the guidelines yet.

00:24:21.740 --> 00:24:26.060
Speaker 3: I think the people who are writing those guidelines are waiting for better data to come out

00:24:25.800 --> 00:24:27.480
Speaker 3: And I guess I am too.

00:24:27.640 --> 00:24:31.080
Speaker 3: But I do know some people who are early adopters who are already going with it.

00:24:31.160 --> 00:24:33.800
Speaker 3: Uh so it'll be interesting to see how that plays out.

00:24:34.140 --> 00:24:39.180
Speaker 1: Yeah, let's talk a little bit about why you would use ketamine in status epilepticus in the first place.

00:24:39.340 --> 00:24:41.980
Speaker 1: So first of all, we're very familiar with ketamine.

00:24:42.220 --> 00:24:43.020
Speaker 1: We use it all the time.

00:24:43.180 --> 00:24:44.540
Speaker 1: So that's that's a plus.

00:24:44.840 --> 00:24:46.919
Speaker 1: It has a fast onset.

00:24:47.080 --> 00:24:48.440
Speaker 1: It's great for airway.

00:24:48.520 --> 00:24:53.799
Speaker 1: Like if you are going to be intubating this patient, it's great for keeping that airway open and keeping them breathing.

00:24:54.160 --> 00:24:55.920
Speaker 1: So that's a nice benefit.

00:24:56.160 --> 00:25:00.480
Speaker 1: And my understanding is that it has pretty good anti-seizure properties.

00:25:00.640 --> 00:25:06.000
Speaker 1: Maybe you could talk a little bit about the pharmacology of ketamine for stopping seizures.

00:25:06.519 --> 00:25:07.320
Speaker 3: Absolutely.

00:25:07.559 --> 00:25:18.200
Speaker 3: And because I think this is an important concept, we know when patients have status epilepticus that a good chunk of them become benzodiazepine resistant

00:25:18.160 --> 00:25:22.960
Speaker 3: It happens in almost a third of cases where benzos stop working.

00:25:23.200 --> 00:25:26.240
Speaker 3: Your GABA receptors essentially become resistant to it.

00:25:26.400 --> 00:25:31.600
Speaker 3: And at that same time, your NMDA receptors are upregulating.

00:25:31.740 --> 00:25:35.340
Speaker 3: And this is more likely to happen as the seizure goes on.

00:25:35.580 --> 00:25:38.940
Speaker 3: And ketamine works by blocking NMDA.

00:25:39.100 --> 00:25:44.299
Speaker 3: So it's actually hitting a second mechanism in your brain that can help stop the seizure.

00:25:44.460 --> 00:25:51.419
Speaker 3: So it makes good sense to me in terms of the pathophysiology about why this may be effective.

00:25:51.740 --> 00:25:54.460
Speaker 3: And I agree with you, this is a safe drug.

00:25:54.620 --> 00:25:56.460
Speaker 3: It's a drug we're used to.

00:25:56.860 --> 00:25:59.580
Speaker 3: It does require a little thought.

00:25:59.740 --> 00:26:02.540
Speaker 3: Like if you use it as your second-line drug.

00:26:02.660 --> 00:26:07.540
Speaker 3: So say I'm giving a milligram per kilo along with my benzos up front in those first few minutes.

00:26:07.700 --> 00:26:13.220
Speaker 3: So I give somebody 70 of ketamine along with the benzos, and they continue to seize

00:26:13.740 --> 00:26:18.060
Speaker 3: What dose are you then giving as your RSI drug if you're going to use that?

00:26:18.220 --> 00:26:19.340
Speaker 3: Are you repeating it?

00:26:19.500 --> 00:26:20.300
Speaker 3: Are you not?

00:26:20.460 --> 00:26:24.220
Speaker 3: What are the pros and cons of repeat doses of ketamine in the short term?

00:26:24.540 --> 00:26:26.940
Speaker 3: which isn't an area we've studied a lot.

00:26:27.100 --> 00:26:33.180
Speaker 3: But you just want to be thinking about what are these downstream consequences if the patient doesn't turn off.

00:26:33.420 --> 00:26:38.140
Speaker 3: But certainly I think knowing that benzodiazepine resistance is real.

00:26:38.820 --> 00:26:41.780
Speaker 3: And having something in your algorithm for that is useful.

00:26:41.860 --> 00:26:44.100
Speaker 3: So ketamine is in my algorithm.

00:26:44.260 --> 00:26:46.180
Speaker 3: It's just not second line

00:26:46.640 --> 00:26:47.200
Speaker 1: Fair enough.

00:26:47.280 --> 00:26:52.000
Speaker 1: It's an interesting point about you've given 70 or 100 milligrams of ketamine.

00:26:52.320 --> 00:26:54.720
Speaker 1: Now you think, oh, I better intubate this patient.

00:26:54.860 --> 00:26:56.460
Speaker 1: What are you gonna do next?

00:27:00.539 --> 00:27:08.700
Speaker 1: So we just talked about using ketamine as a second line medication, but secondline just meaning that with your second benzodose.

00:27:09.100 --> 00:27:19.100
Speaker 1: There are multiple options for your second-line seizure medication, and these all include your sort of typical anti-seizure medications.

00:27:19.440 --> 00:27:26.000
Speaker 1: And I would say probably the biggest pitfall in using these medications is just not giving them early enough.

00:27:26.159 --> 00:27:30.480
Speaker 1: I mean, similar to the benzos, is that we that we think of them as kind of an afterthought.

00:27:30.820 --> 00:27:35.540
Speaker 1: And most of them have a fairly long onset of action.

00:27:35.780 --> 00:27:43.940
Speaker 1: And so we really want to give them as early as possible because they aren't going to start working for another five minutes or 10 minutes or 20 minutes, depending on which drug you use.

00:27:44.000 --> 00:27:45.360
Speaker 1: So there's a whole bunch of options here.

00:27:45.440 --> 00:27:51.280
Speaker 1: There's phenytoin, there's levitoracetem or kepra, there's velproate.

00:27:51.680 --> 00:27:53.280
Speaker 1: First, the timing.

00:27:53.380 --> 00:27:56.180
Speaker 1: We had mentioned ketamine with your second dose of benzo.

00:27:56.340 --> 00:28:00.500
Speaker 1: When are you going to give this so-called second-line seizure medication?

00:28:00.660 --> 00:28:02.500
Speaker 1: Which one would you give and why?

00:28:02.900 --> 00:28:03.220
Speaker 3: Okay.

00:28:04.179 --> 00:28:11.779
Speaker 3: For my first five minutes of the patient seizing, if I've started a clock, I'm only giving benzos in the first five minutes.

00:28:11.660 --> 00:28:20.300
Speaker 3: But once they cross the five-minute mark for me, they've hit the definition of status, and I'm gonna add a second line drug right there and then.

00:28:20.460 --> 00:28:25.820
Speaker 3: So this is where you get to choose whether you want Phenetoin, Valproic, Levatir CTEM, etc.

00:28:26.220 --> 00:28:27.740
Speaker 3: And there are pros and cons.

00:28:27.820 --> 00:28:29.020
Speaker 3: We can talk through some of those.

00:28:29.180 --> 00:28:31.900
Speaker 3: The old school option was to give phenytoin.

00:28:32.240 --> 00:28:37.040
Speaker 3: which I find has largely fallen out of favor because it takes so long to give it.

00:28:37.200 --> 00:28:42.720
Speaker 3: Most phenytoin or phosphenytoin infusions have to go in over about twenty minutes minimum.

00:28:42.860 --> 00:28:47.899
Speaker 3: And because that slows down the onset so much, I think it's fallen out of favor as an option.

00:28:48.139 --> 00:28:56.700
Speaker 3: So people are more likely, I think, these days, to use either Levatyra cetem or lecosamide, which can go in quite rapidly.

00:28:56.820 --> 00:29:02.260
Speaker 3: So Levatira cetam, you're giving patients 60 milligrams per kilo.

00:29:02.340 --> 00:29:04.180
Speaker 3: You're giving them a big dose.

00:29:04.340 --> 00:29:06.100
Speaker 3: And this is up to a maximum of 4.

00:29:06.260 --> 00:29:07.380
Speaker 3: 5 grams.

00:29:07.640 --> 00:29:11.240
Speaker 3: It can go safely over five minutes IV.

00:29:11.400 --> 00:29:16.280
Speaker 3: There have been a lot of recent studies showing that is safe and feasible for patients.

00:29:16.540 --> 00:29:21.820
Speaker 3: And so that gets it into your patient in a timely fashion where it might actually start working.

00:29:22.060 --> 00:29:30.700
Speaker 3: You do just want to wrap your mind around that dose for a second, because most people who we see as outpatients on Leviteracetam are maybe on a gram a day.

00:29:30.300 --> 00:29:32.060
Speaker 3: And we're talking about giving 4.

00:29:32.300 --> 00:29:34.620
Speaker 3: 5 grams in five minutes.

00:29:34.940 --> 00:29:40.060
Speaker 3: So mentally just adjusting to the fact that you're going to give a whopper dose to these patients is useful.

00:29:40.340 --> 00:29:46.020
Speaker 3: But we know that if they're in status, benzos alone aren't enough.

00:29:46.179 --> 00:29:51.860
Speaker 3: Like we want the benzo to try to turn it off, but they're still gonna need a longer-term maintenance anti-seizure medicine

00:29:52.060 --> 00:29:54.860
Speaker 3: So you're gonna want to give one of these second-line drugs.

00:29:55.020 --> 00:29:56.700
Speaker 3: So I like levatura cetam.

00:29:56.780 --> 00:29:58.220
Speaker 3: That's what we use the most at my place.

00:29:58.620 --> 00:29:58.940
Speaker 1: Okay.

00:29:59.100 --> 00:30:03.100
Speaker 1: We have a lot of patients who take, I call it levaturacetam.

00:30:03.360 --> 00:30:04.720
Speaker 1: I'm not sure what the correct pronunciation is.

00:30:04.960 --> 00:30:05.279
Speaker 1: I don't know.

00:30:05.519 --> 00:30:06.480
Speaker 3: Tomato tomato.

00:30:06.720 --> 00:30:07.360
Speaker 1: Yeah, I guess so.

00:30:07.519 --> 00:30:08.399
Speaker 1: Potato potato.

00:30:08.639 --> 00:30:09.840
Speaker 3: It's definitely hard to say.

00:30:10.080 --> 00:30:10.559
Speaker 1: Yeah.

00:30:12.340 --> 00:30:14.179
Speaker 1: I'm gonna call it levituracetem.

00:30:14.340 --> 00:30:17.380
Speaker 1: Or we could call it Kepra too, but I guess we're supposed to keep to the generic name.

00:30:19.299 --> 00:30:20.500
Speaker 3: I'm trying to be good.

00:30:21.059 --> 00:30:25.700
Speaker 1: So for levitar let's say the patient comes in on levituracetem, because actually that's

00:30:25.720 --> 00:30:30.120
Speaker 1: That's quite a few patients who we see with status epilepticus.

00:30:30.200 --> 00:30:32.600
Speaker 1: A lot of them will be on leviterastatem.

00:30:33.000 --> 00:30:35.240
Speaker 1: We don't know if they're compliant with their medications or not.

00:30:35.320 --> 00:30:38.520
Speaker 1: I mean, that might be one of the reasons why they're having why they're having a seizure.

00:30:38.640 --> 00:30:43.280
Speaker 1: But in the real world, someone comes in, you know they're on levatoracetem.

00:30:43.440 --> 00:30:49.760
Speaker 1: How's that gonna change your management in terms of are you gonna choose a different medication or are you gonna choose levatoracetem?

00:30:49.920 --> 00:30:53.200
Speaker 1: Are you gonna adjust the dose if you know that they are on leviteracetem?

00:30:54.059 --> 00:31:03.260
Speaker 3: So in the heat of the moment, even if they are on Levitaracitam, I'm not going to be able to check a level and get a level back in a timely manner.

00:31:03.419 --> 00:31:05.500
Speaker 3: That's not feasible at my place.

00:31:05.560 --> 00:31:08.920
Speaker 3: I am gonna still just give them the 60 per kilo.

00:31:09.080 --> 00:31:14.360
Speaker 3: So for the average, you know, person, that's gonna be in the ballpark of four point five grams

00:31:14.360 --> 00:31:15.880
Speaker 3: It's still safe for them.

00:31:16.120 --> 00:31:17.320
Speaker 3: It is effective.

00:31:17.559 --> 00:31:22.840
Speaker 3: I want to turn off whatever is happening in their brain, so I don't dose adjust.

00:31:22.800 --> 00:31:24.000
Speaker 1: Okay, that makes it simple.

00:31:24.160 --> 00:31:31.520
Speaker 1: So whether they're on levaturacetem or not, your first line is levaturacetem and it's 60 milligrams per kilogram.

00:31:31.600 --> 00:31:33.040
Speaker 1: It's a huge dose.

00:31:33.340 --> 00:31:34.940
Speaker 1: up to four and a half grams.

00:31:35.100 --> 00:31:38.700
Speaker 1: Most adults are going to take like four grams kind of dosing.

00:31:39.020 --> 00:31:44.140
Speaker 1: We're going to have an algorithm, again, an updated algorithm on the EMKes website.

00:31:44.260 --> 00:31:49.380
Speaker 1: that you can access fairly quickly just by typing in EM cases, status epilepticus.

00:31:49.700 --> 00:31:53.220
Speaker 3: And this is why it's valuable to just like because you do have options here.

00:31:53.380 --> 00:31:57.460
Speaker 3: So pick your favorite and learn that one drug and that dose.

00:31:57.840 --> 00:31:58.080
Speaker 3: Right?

00:31:58.160 --> 00:32:08.799
Speaker 3: Rather than trying to remember all four, pick the one you use most often or whatever's stocked and most easily feasible in your department, because it can be really tricky in the heat of the moment to remember all those doses.

00:32:18.740 --> 00:32:23.220
Speaker 1: The other options for second-line medications, you had mentioned one

00:32:23.260 --> 00:32:33.660
Speaker 1: Lucosamide, which I know nothing about, but it's on the list there, and apparently it's a very good medication and it works quite quickly.

00:32:33.860 --> 00:32:39.220
Speaker 1: You can also give it over just five minutes rather than phenytoin, which takes at least 20.

00:32:39.539 --> 00:32:41.779
Speaker 1: What do we need to know about leucosamide?

00:32:42.019 --> 00:32:43.380
Speaker 1: Is it even widely available?

00:32:43.539 --> 00:32:45.860
Speaker 1: Is it better than levitaracetem?

00:32:46.000 --> 00:32:47.680
Speaker 1: When would you use it in your algorithm?

00:32:47.840 --> 00:32:49.280
Speaker 1: Tell us a little bit about that drug.

00:32:49.600 --> 00:32:50.640
Speaker 3: Okay, good questions.

00:32:50.880 --> 00:32:54.080
Speaker 3: Lucosamide is a great second line option.

00:32:54.240 --> 00:32:56.800
Speaker 3: You can give it over five minutes, as you mentioned.

00:32:56.880 --> 00:32:59.360
Speaker 3: Uh the dose is 400 milligrams IV.

00:32:59.540 --> 00:33:11.540
Speaker 3: There's no data that it's better or worse, and really we're extrapolating data here about our second line options from the ESET trial, where they showed that they are all roughly equivalent in their efficacy

00:33:12.179 --> 00:33:17.860
Speaker 3: For me, the reason why I prefer Levatira CTM is purely from feasibility.

00:33:18.100 --> 00:33:21.539
Speaker 3: We don't stock leucosamide in my emergency department.

00:33:21.620 --> 00:33:25.779
Speaker 3: So I have to get it down from the main pharmacy, which just takes longer.

00:33:25.660 --> 00:33:28.380
Speaker 3: And so that's why it's not my top choice.

00:33:28.540 --> 00:33:33.740
Speaker 3: But if it's something you have readily available, certainly reasonable to go ahead with it

00:33:33.700 --> 00:33:38.659
Speaker 3: There are a couple things you want to watch out for in terms of potential side effects.

00:33:38.980 --> 00:33:43.700
Speaker 3: You don't want to use it in the patient who has a known heart block or conduction system disease.

00:33:43.860 --> 00:33:47.139
Speaker 3: There have been reported cases of V-TAC with glucosamide.

00:33:47.060 --> 00:33:49.540
Speaker 3: So you want to be thoughtful about arrhythmias.

00:33:49.940 --> 00:33:54.580
Speaker 3: You also want to avoid it in your patient with severe hepatic dysfunction.

00:33:54.500 --> 00:34:01.380
Speaker 3: And so think about that in your ethanol use patients and other groups, just because those are the two places where you want to avoid it.

00:34:01.620 --> 00:34:03.540
Speaker 3: Arrhythmias and liver disease.

00:34:03.740 --> 00:34:03.980
Speaker 1: Okay.

00:34:04.300 --> 00:34:07.900
Speaker 1: And in some algorithms, leucosamide is used as sort of a third line agent.

00:34:07.980 --> 00:34:11.260
Speaker 1: Like let's say you've given Levitoracetem and they're still seizing.

00:34:11.359 --> 00:34:15.359
Speaker 1: Lycosamide is is then in some algorithms to add on.

00:34:15.520 --> 00:34:18.960
Speaker 1: Okay, so good to know about heart blocks and liver disease.

00:34:19.200 --> 00:34:20.320
Speaker 1: for leucosamide.

00:34:20.560 --> 00:34:27.359
Speaker 1: Let's talk about after your second line medication has gone in, your second dose of benzo has gone in, the patient's still

00:34:27.840 --> 00:34:28.640
Speaker 1: seizing.

00:34:28.880 --> 00:34:32.240
Speaker 1: Now we're into refractory status.

00:34:32.400 --> 00:34:34.240
Speaker 1: We're thinking about airway now.

00:34:34.400 --> 00:34:36.240
Speaker 1: Let's start with airway consideration.

00:34:36.400 --> 00:34:36.720
Speaker 1: So

00:34:37.240 --> 00:34:39.399
Speaker 1: Again, this is all about timing.

00:34:39.639 --> 00:34:43.639
Speaker 1: When do you decide to intubate the patient with status epilepticus?

00:34:43.800 --> 00:34:45.480
Speaker 1: And if you do intubate

00:34:45.740 --> 00:34:47.260
Speaker 1: Are you using propofol?

00:34:47.419 --> 00:34:48.379
Speaker 1: Are you using ketamine?

00:34:48.540 --> 00:34:50.860
Speaker 1: Are you using ketopol as your induction agent?

00:34:50.940 --> 00:34:54.460
Speaker 1: And then there's all kinds of controversy around the paralytic agent.

00:34:54.760 --> 00:35:01.880
Speaker 1: Because rock eronium is long-acting and you don't want to obliterate your assessment of whether they're in a seizure or not.

00:35:02.040 --> 00:35:04.600
Speaker 1: And then there's there's a reversal agent for that.

00:35:04.680 --> 00:35:07.240
Speaker 1: Um so if you have that, so it gets complicated.

00:35:07.200 --> 00:35:08.720
Speaker 1: But let's try and make it simple.

00:35:08.960 --> 00:35:10.560
Speaker 1: When do you decide to intubate?

00:35:10.800 --> 00:35:15.599
Speaker 1: If you do decide to intubate, what induction agent are you using and what paralytic are you using?

00:35:16.480 --> 00:35:17.440
Speaker 3: Good question.

00:35:17.680 --> 00:35:19.040
Speaker 3: So let's recap.

00:35:19.520 --> 00:35:22.160
Speaker 3: My first five minutes are really just benzos.

00:35:22.560 --> 00:35:29.680
Speaker 3: The second five minutes, which is between five to ten minutes of their seizure, I'm still giving benzos and I'm adding my second line drug.

00:35:30.480 --> 00:35:39.440
Speaker 3: In the third five minutes, which is between 10 to 15 minutes now that they're seizing, I'm giving third-line drugs as I intubate

00:35:40.000 --> 00:35:46.960
Speaker 3: And so I tend to go with propofol, although you could also choose ketamine here.

00:35:47.120 --> 00:35:50.480
Speaker 3: There isn't data to suggest that one is better than the other.

00:35:50.840 --> 00:35:55.720
Speaker 3: You do want to think about the blood pressure when you're deciding on your induction agent.

00:35:55.960 --> 00:36:01.320
Speaker 3: If their blood pressure was borderline, that may push me to use ketamine rather than propofol.

00:36:01.760 --> 00:36:10.720
Speaker 3: Although I would say in general, these patients are going to need massive doses of sedation to try to ensure they stop seizing.

00:36:10.859 --> 00:36:14.460
Speaker 3: And they all end up going onto a presser like norepinephrine.

00:36:14.700 --> 00:36:24.300
Speaker 3: So it's not unreasonable to start a presser at this stage to facilitate the whopper doses you're going to want to keep giving for their sedation, even after they're intubated.

00:36:24.620 --> 00:36:28.060
Speaker 3: But certainly think about the blood pressure as you're choosing your agent.

00:36:28.300 --> 00:36:33.420
Speaker 3: And the guidelines still suggest that we do do an RSI so that we're giving these patients a paralytic.

00:36:33.559 --> 00:36:37.000
Speaker 3: The upside to this is it improves your intubating conditions, so we like that.

00:36:37.079 --> 00:36:39.480
Speaker 3: We want to optimize for first pass success.

00:36:39.880 --> 00:36:46.359
Speaker 3: The downside is you will no longer be able to see clinically if your patient is still seizing.

00:36:46.660 --> 00:36:53.380
Speaker 3: And this is a problem because most of us do not have access to bedside EEG to have a different marker.

00:36:53.700 --> 00:36:56.260
Speaker 3: So your paralytic choices are tricky.

00:36:56.640 --> 00:36:59.839
Speaker 3: So let me lay out the options for you and you can think about what you would do.

00:37:00.000 --> 00:37:02.960
Speaker 3: Number one, you could go old school and give succinylcholine.

00:37:03.200 --> 00:37:04.079
Speaker 3: Easy to do.

00:37:04.319 --> 00:37:09.599
Speaker 3: And the upside of choosing this is that it's going to be gone in about five minutes.

00:37:09.480 --> 00:37:15.000
Speaker 3: And that way if your patient is still seizing, you'll be able to see it clinically and titrate your sedation to that.

00:37:15.160 --> 00:37:20.680
Speaker 3: So there's value here, I do think, in bringing back sucks in selected patients.

00:37:20.859 --> 00:37:34.060
Speaker 3: You want to be mindful about the contraindications to succinylcholine, which obviously include malignant hyperthermia, but we worry about hypercholemic reactions in patients who have been seizing.

00:37:34.500 --> 00:37:43.220
Speaker 3: Particularly they've been seizing for a long time, and that's usually upwards of twenty to twenty-five to thirty minutes, like something in that ballpark.

00:37:43.260 --> 00:37:47.340
Speaker 3: So this is another good reason to think about controlling the airway early.

00:37:47.500 --> 00:37:50.940
Speaker 3: If they've been seizing for half an hour, I probably wouldn't give sucks.

00:37:51.000 --> 00:38:00.760
Speaker 3: But if I'm still around that 10-15 minute mark where I like to be thinking about this, it is safe to do as long as you're clear on the onset time of the seizure

00:38:00.920 --> 00:38:06.600
Speaker 1: Great point about considering how long the seizure's been going on in your decision to use sucks or not.

00:38:07.000 --> 00:38:07.480
Speaker 3: Yes.

00:38:07.640 --> 00:38:13.800
Speaker 3: And the time when I really want to carefully cross-check that is when they've started seizing in the field.

00:38:14.140 --> 00:38:14.380
Speaker 3: Right?

00:38:14.539 --> 00:38:16.940
Speaker 3: Because it's not just the time they've been seizing in front of me.

00:38:17.020 --> 00:38:19.099
Speaker 3: I want to really know the onset time.

00:38:19.420 --> 00:38:23.500
Speaker 3: If I'm not sure, then I'm going to default to using Rockuronium.

00:38:23.760 --> 00:38:25.840
Speaker 3: Because it's going to give us great intubating conditions.

00:38:26.000 --> 00:38:29.600
Speaker 3: The downside is it's sticking around for half an hour or so.

00:38:30.000 --> 00:38:33.920
Speaker 3: And so to manage that half hour, you have options

00:38:34.220 --> 00:38:42.700
Speaker 3: One is just to assume your patient is still seizing, even though you can't see it, and use maximal doses of sedation to try to stop it.

00:38:43.640 --> 00:38:48.200
Speaker 3: One is to reverse your rock uronium with Segamodex.

00:38:48.360 --> 00:38:51.480
Speaker 3: And I don't know how quickly you can actually get it to the bedside.

00:38:51.560 --> 00:38:53.400
Speaker 3: This is variable across centers.

00:38:53.460 --> 00:39:00.820
Speaker 3: but it may not actually buy you a ton here in terms of clinical visualization of the seizure.

00:39:01.520 --> 00:39:15.680
Speaker 3: So my preferred option, I must say, Anton, is super old school, but when I am using rock uronium, I will put a tourniquet around whatever limb is seizing most visibly.

00:39:15.920 --> 00:39:25.680
Speaker 3: Because then your rock uronium circulates to the airway and gives you the airway conditions you're looking for, but doesn't circulate to that limb, and I can still see clinically if they're seizing.

00:39:26.140 --> 00:39:27.660
Speaker 1: Sarah MacGyver Gray.

00:39:28.460 --> 00:39:28.779
Speaker 3: Right?

00:39:29.020 --> 00:39:32.619
Speaker 3: It's just it's just this is an old, old anesthesia trick.

00:39:32.700 --> 00:39:34.460
Speaker 3: This is not this was not my idea.

00:39:34.700 --> 00:39:38.940
Speaker 3: I was taught this as a medical student uh by a clever intensivist.

00:39:39.339 --> 00:39:40.460
Speaker 1: There's a pearl.

00:39:40.440 --> 00:39:40.920
Speaker 3: Right?

00:39:41.160 --> 00:39:51.079
Speaker 3: But so and you can easily keep your tourniquet up for 15 minutes, 20 minutes, half an hour, like you've got some time there, and you'll be able to see if they're seizing.

00:39:51.059 --> 00:39:54.099
Speaker 3: Put it on whichever limb the seizure is most visible in.

00:39:54.260 --> 00:39:56.020
Speaker 3: This is not a perfect fix, right?

00:39:56.099 --> 00:40:03.220
Speaker 3: I would I would much prefer to have continuous live EEG monitoring all the time, but I don't live in that world

00:40:03.340 --> 00:40:05.340
Speaker 3: So old school is a tourniquet.

00:40:05.900 --> 00:40:10.300
Speaker 1: I mean for me, you know, I I used sucks for many years and I'm pretty comfortable with it.

00:40:10.460 --> 00:40:11.820
Speaker 1: So for me, it's a no-brainer.

00:40:11.900 --> 00:40:13.500
Speaker 1: Just use sucks, you're good.

00:40:13.480 --> 00:40:14.279
Speaker 3: Yeah, agreed.

00:40:14.440 --> 00:40:14.680
Speaker 3: Right.

00:40:14.760 --> 00:40:18.359
Speaker 3: And that's, I gotta say, that's my go-to if the onset is clear.

00:40:18.520 --> 00:40:24.039
Speaker 3: If I have any uncertainty about the onset or they've been seizing a long time, then I'm going rock uronium with a tourniquet.

00:40:24.240 --> 00:40:24.720
Speaker 2: Great.

