00:41:18.380 --> 00:41:21.900
Speaker 1: So that's the paralytic and the induction agent.

00:41:21.940 --> 00:41:24.579
Speaker 1: Let's say we've given a few doses of benzos.

00:41:24.740 --> 00:41:26.820
Speaker 1: Maybe we've given ketamine or not.

00:41:26.980 --> 00:41:28.500
Speaker 1: We've now intubated.

00:41:28.579 --> 00:41:32.260
Speaker 1: We've given propofol and sucks, let's say.

00:41:32.420 --> 00:41:35.220
Speaker 1: Let's say we've started a propofol drip.

00:41:35.160 --> 00:41:41.240
Speaker 1: Let's talk a little bit about that, about your third-line medication after intubation.

00:41:41.400 --> 00:41:44.520
Speaker 1: What kind of dosages are you using for propofol?

00:41:44.760 --> 00:41:45.480
Speaker 1: Let's start with.

00:41:45.600 --> 00:41:46.800
Speaker 1: And what are your other options?

00:41:46.880 --> 00:41:50.080
Speaker 1: There's you know phenobarbital, there's more benzos.

00:41:50.320 --> 00:41:51.440
Speaker 1: What do you suggest?

00:41:51.760 --> 00:41:58.720
Speaker 3: Yeah, so first line for me is benzos, which I'm giving repeatedly every three to five minutes until I intubate.

00:41:58.859 --> 00:42:01.099
Speaker 3: Second line, I'm giving Levitir CTM.

00:42:01.420 --> 00:42:08.140
Speaker 3: Third line for me is whatever my induction medication is for my intubation, whether it's ketamine or propofol.

00:42:08.559 --> 00:42:16.640
Speaker 3: And then I'm starting, and typically I will say for me it's propofol, and then I'm starting a propofol infusion post intubation.

00:42:16.760 --> 00:42:20.440
Speaker 3: and thinking about going with relatively high doses.

00:42:20.680 --> 00:42:26.520
Speaker 3: If you use milligrams per kilo per hour, that's usually sort of in the range of five to ten milligrams per kilo per hour

00:42:26.760 --> 00:42:33.800
Speaker 3: If you do mics per kilo per minute, your dosing will be different, usually up to sort of 80 or 100 mics per kilo per minute.

00:42:33.960 --> 00:42:36.760
Speaker 3: So it's worth knowing how the pumps work at your place.

00:42:36.840 --> 00:42:38.440
Speaker 3: That usually is what dictates.

00:42:38.559 --> 00:42:46.960
Speaker 3: your infusion, but you want to be at the high end of the range, and that's often with norepinephrine to support the blood pressure

00:42:47.320 --> 00:42:55.640
Speaker 1: Okay, that's I think a key point there that m is sort of a key pitfall is that if we just give sort of our usual sedation doses of propofol.

00:42:56.160 --> 00:43:04.160
Speaker 1: you know, post-intubation sedation doses of of propofol, we will be underdosing for status epilepticus for sure.

00:43:04.320 --> 00:43:06.240
Speaker 1: So these are much higher doses.

00:43:06.320 --> 00:43:08.640
Speaker 1: This is something that you need to pause and look up.

00:43:08.859 --> 00:43:14.619
Speaker 1: And make sure you're getting the right dose and that it's a high dose higher than your usual sedation doses.

00:43:14.940 --> 00:43:15.260
Speaker 3: Yeah.

00:43:15.420 --> 00:43:19.820
Speaker 3: Like I'll tell you, Anton, until I can get an EEG there

00:43:20.059 --> 00:43:24.619
Speaker 3: Which I will tell you, even at my place, is an awfully long time until I get an EEG.

00:43:25.420 --> 00:43:28.380
Speaker 3: I really want to turn off that seizure.

00:43:28.460 --> 00:43:32.859
Speaker 3: And so my default is to give them the maximum dose of propofol.

00:43:33.420 --> 00:43:33.740
Speaker 1: Okay.

00:43:34.420 --> 00:43:37.620
Speaker 1: And you'll almost certainly be starting norepinephrine.

00:43:37.780 --> 00:43:38.100
Speaker 1: Yes.

00:43:38.340 --> 00:43:39.460
Speaker 1: So have that ready.

00:43:39.620 --> 00:43:46.820
Speaker 1: Have your norepinephrine drip ready to go as soon as you're starting your infusion because pretty much all of these patients, I assume.

00:43:47.140 --> 00:43:50.099
Speaker 1: will have a low blood pressure and require pressure support.

00:43:50.420 --> 00:43:50.900
Speaker 3: Absolutely.

00:43:51.140 --> 00:43:57.380
Speaker 3: I'm actually typically asking for my nore epi when I'm getting induction drugs for my intubation.

00:43:57.640 --> 00:43:59.640
Speaker 3: Because they've already had benzose.

00:43:59.720 --> 00:44:01.559
Speaker 3: I'm gonna give them a dose of propofol.

00:44:01.799 --> 00:44:05.799
Speaker 3: Uh the risk of hypotension is very high, and I like to be proactive about that.

00:44:05.960 --> 00:44:09.240
Speaker 3: So I typically intubate with norepi hanging in the room.

00:44:09.359 --> 00:44:12.480
Speaker 3: or at least some version of push dose presser available in the room.

00:44:12.640 --> 00:44:16.960
Speaker 3: And then I'm gonna put them on a high dose propofol infusion and norepia at the same time.

00:44:17.200 --> 00:44:17.520
Speaker 3: Okay.

00:44:17.920 --> 00:44:18.640
Speaker 1: That's a good point.

00:44:18.800 --> 00:44:22.560
Speaker 1: I mean we should probably just have norepinephrine ready for every intubation we do.

00:44:22.640 --> 00:44:24.320
Speaker 1: So it's just easy to reach for.

00:44:24.640 --> 00:44:27.600
Speaker 3: Yeah, it's part we've made it part of our intubation order set now.

00:44:27.680 --> 00:44:28.880
Speaker 3: It's all bundled together.

00:44:29.059 --> 00:44:32.420
Speaker 3: When you click for your intubation drugs, you get your norepi every time.

00:44:32.819 --> 00:44:33.140
Speaker 1: Great.

00:44:33.380 --> 00:44:38.900
Speaker 1: Yeah, maybe at the end we'll talk a little bit about sort of human factors and logistics of how to do all of this because we've

00:44:39.260 --> 00:44:43.420
Speaker 1: We're covering a lot of ground here, uh, and there's a lot of little moving parts.

00:44:43.580 --> 00:44:52.460
Speaker 1: Then there's going to be logistics and human factors to make sure that this all works with all the timing that you want, because again, this is all about good fast timing.

00:44:52.840 --> 00:44:55.160
Speaker 1: You had mentioned EEG monitoring.

00:44:55.480 --> 00:44:58.760
Speaker 1: I hear there's all kinds of new technology for EEG monitoring.

00:44:58.840 --> 00:45:00.360
Speaker 1: That sounds all exciting.

00:45:00.460 --> 00:45:03.420
Speaker 1: What do we need to know about EEG monitoring?

00:45:03.580 --> 00:45:11.580
Speaker 1: I'm assuming that 99% of our listeners cannot get EEG monitoring within 30 minutes of a patient seizing in your emergency department.

00:45:11.840 --> 00:45:16.720
Speaker 1: What's the kind of cool thing that's coming down the pipes in terms of seizure monitoring?

00:45:17.280 --> 00:45:23.840
Speaker 3: Yeah, so uh for status epilepticus you really want continuous EEG monitoring?

00:45:24.140 --> 00:45:28.700
Speaker 3: which is not routinely available, I would say, in most emergency departments.

00:45:28.940 --> 00:45:32.700
Speaker 3: Certainly in Canadian ADs, it's essentially impossible.

00:45:32.840 --> 00:45:34.840
Speaker 3: And tricky, frankly, for some ICUs.

00:45:35.000 --> 00:45:37.560
Speaker 3: Some ICUs can't get continuous EEG either.

00:45:37.880 --> 00:45:41.640
Speaker 3: But artificial intelligence is changing all of this.

00:45:42.040 --> 00:45:51.720
Speaker 3: There is currently a device available in the US that's FDA approved that uses, it's sort of like a headband that you strap on the patient's forehead.

00:45:51.840 --> 00:45:58.000
Speaker 3: And AI interprets the EEG readings and you get a diagnosis within five minutes or so.

00:45:58.320 --> 00:46:00.640
Speaker 3: So currently available

00:46:00.859 --> 00:46:03.660
Speaker 3: Testing for it is ongoing, but looks really promising.

00:46:03.740 --> 00:46:09.180
Speaker 3: Like the data has shown it's just as accurate as being read by a neurologist.

00:46:09.359 --> 00:46:12.800
Speaker 3: you know, people are limited right now, often in terms of price point.

00:46:12.960 --> 00:46:15.599
Speaker 3: Uh my understanding is that it's very expensive.

00:46:15.760 --> 00:46:22.560
Speaker 3: But I think technology like this is going to be a game changer, at least as soon as it's affordable enough that we all have one.

00:46:22.640 --> 00:46:28.960
Speaker 3: Uh because then you're gonna know when the seizure stops and can titrate your medications effectively.

00:46:29.440 --> 00:46:32.960
Speaker 1: I'm assuming you don't have stocks and have a yacht that you've bought based on your medicine.

00:46:33.279 --> 00:46:34.400
Speaker 3: If only Yeah, no.

00:46:34.559 --> 00:46:37.200
Speaker 3: I can't I can't honestly I can't even remember the

00:46:37.559 --> 00:46:40.440
Speaker 3: The name of the commercial device, the name of the company's escaping me.

00:46:40.520 --> 00:46:42.680
Speaker 3: I have no stocks or conflict of interest.

00:46:43.000 --> 00:46:47.240
Speaker 3: Yeah, I mean, I frankly, I hope they hear this and send us one to try, Anton.

00:46:47.319 --> 00:46:48.359
Speaker 3: Wouldn't that be awesome?

00:46:49.359 --> 00:46:50.480
Speaker 3: Whoever you are out there.

00:46:50.880 --> 00:46:52.400
Speaker 3: This is a shout out into the ether.

00:46:52.640 --> 00:46:54.640
Speaker 3: We would love to test your machine.

00:46:56.560 --> 00:46:57.359
Speaker 1: It's kind of cool.

00:46:57.440 --> 00:46:58.160
Speaker 1: The headband thing.

00:46:58.240 --> 00:47:03.040
Speaker 1: You know, we were talking about a headband thing in our cardiac arrest episode that we did a couple of years back.

00:47:02.980 --> 00:47:03.140
Speaker 1: Yeah.

00:47:03.539 --> 00:47:12.819
Speaker 1: I can just imagine in in in a decade from now that all our critically ill patients are gonna look like Bjorn Borg if anyone's old enough listening to to know who Beyond Borg was.

00:47:13.240 --> 00:47:15.400
Speaker 1: Uh they're gonna look like, you know, having that headband.

00:47:15.960 --> 00:47:16.280
Speaker 3: Right.

00:47:16.440 --> 00:47:18.840
Speaker 3: Well, I mean, and I actually think you're totally true.

00:47:18.920 --> 00:47:22.840
Speaker 3: Like there are all sorts of ICU devices that have been tested that way, things to measure.

00:47:23.140 --> 00:47:29.460
Speaker 3: oxygen saturation and jugular venous return and various other sort of markers of neurologic health.

00:47:29.539 --> 00:47:31.220
Speaker 3: Uh like I think this is coming.

00:47:31.380 --> 00:47:32.740
Speaker 3: So I think that's super exciting.

00:47:32.900 --> 00:47:36.339
Speaker 3: Like I think when we have these in I don't know how many years

00:47:36.359 --> 00:47:38.200
Speaker 3: It's going to completely change our approach.

00:47:38.599 --> 00:47:39.320
Speaker 1: Amazing.

00:47:39.640 --> 00:47:40.040
Speaker 1: All right.

00:47:40.200 --> 00:47:44.920
Speaker 1: Any other tips or tricks when it comes to managing status epilepticus that we haven't already covered?

00:47:45.079 --> 00:47:46.920
Speaker 1: Maybe some human factor stuff.

00:47:46.960 --> 00:47:50.720
Speaker 1: uh before we get into sort of your algorithm or anything else.

00:47:50.960 --> 00:47:56.960
Speaker 1: I mean one tip I've heard about is and that I do myself is when you're calling for benzos.

00:47:57.540 --> 00:48:03.940
Speaker 1: Call for multiple doses of benzos off the top rather than calling for your first dose and then, oh, that's not working.

00:48:04.100 --> 00:48:07.700
Speaker 1: Let's run back to the machine and get another dose.

00:48:08.040 --> 00:48:19.560
Speaker 1: So that's just one little practical tip is uh when you're calling for your first dose of benzo, ask for at least two doses, if not three, so that you can give them in rapid succession if you need to.

00:48:19.840 --> 00:48:20.640
Speaker 3: Absolutely, yeah.

00:48:20.720 --> 00:48:21.840
Speaker 3: I love that as a tip.

00:48:22.000 --> 00:48:29.520
Speaker 3: And so the first things I ask for, I totally agree, I ask for multiple doses of my Loraspim up front, assuming I have an IV.

00:48:29.840 --> 00:48:34.880
Speaker 3: I want the cap glucose right away, and I want to send that VBG with the electrolytes right away.

00:48:35.040 --> 00:48:38.000
Speaker 3: So those are the first three things I have in the room.

00:48:38.000 --> 00:48:41.760
Speaker 3: And that gives us a few minutes and most seizures are gonna stop there.

00:48:41.920 --> 00:48:46.160
Speaker 3: Like most of the time you're not gonna have to go past those three moves.

00:48:46.400 --> 00:48:52.000
Speaker 3: Maybe now I'll add, grab my ultrasound and make sure my patient isn't pregnant, because we talked about that.

00:48:52.040 --> 00:48:54.520
Speaker 3: But then you have a little bit of time, right?

00:48:54.680 --> 00:49:05.800
Speaker 3: After the five minute mark, I'm adding a second line drug, but it's just the one drug, typically, whichever one of them you want, out of Levatura, Cetam, Valproic, Lucosamide, or Phenytoin.

00:49:06.140 --> 00:49:09.339
Speaker 3: And then you have five minutes there while you're waiting for that to work.

00:49:09.500 --> 00:49:14.460
Speaker 3: You're still giving repeat doses of benzos to think about your airway and get set up for that.

00:49:14.400 --> 00:49:29.119
Speaker 3: So that's your time to call your RT, to get your pressers, to choose your induction drug, to get all that stuff ready, so that if you hit the 10 minute mark and your patient's still seizing, you're ready to move in a timely fashion.

00:49:29.160 --> 00:49:37.000
Speaker 3: So I really like I can't overestimate how important it is to have a timer going in the room and to be really thoughtful about

00:49:37.740 --> 00:49:39.100
Speaker 3: treating this aggressively.

00:49:39.260 --> 00:49:42.380
Speaker 3: Like we have to treat this so much more aggressively than we used to, Anton.

00:49:42.540 --> 00:49:44.940
Speaker 3: We used to have 30 minutes before we got worried.

00:49:45.100 --> 00:49:47.660
Speaker 3: And I I shudder when I think about it, 'cause

00:49:48.220 --> 00:49:50.700
Speaker 3: God, like the outcomes for people were not great.

00:49:50.779 --> 00:49:52.940
Speaker 3: That's why the guidelines have shifted so much.

00:49:53.180 --> 00:49:59.500
Speaker 3: But we now need to be much faster and much more aggressive off the hop to prevent those bad outcomes.

00:50:00.120 --> 00:50:01.240
Speaker 1: Couldn't agree more.

00:50:01.480 --> 00:50:04.120
Speaker 1: I just want to highlight a few things that we've talked about.

00:50:04.440 --> 00:50:08.120
Speaker 1: If you do go with Laraza Pem as your first line, Benzo.

00:50:08.200 --> 00:50:10.359
Speaker 1: The guidelines say four milligrams.

00:50:10.520 --> 00:50:16.200
Speaker 1: You can go as high as eight milligrams for those patients who have known alcohol use disorder.

00:50:16.359 --> 00:50:22.119
Speaker 1: You know, maybe for the patient with a really high BMI, you might want to go with that higher dose.

00:50:22.320 --> 00:50:28.560
Speaker 1: If you do choose midazolam as your first go-to, 5 to 10 milligrams IV.

00:50:28.720 --> 00:50:34.960
Speaker 1: Certainly if you don't have an IV, you're going to be using medazolam 10 milligrams IM intramuscular.

00:50:35.580 --> 00:50:41.340
Speaker 1: You really want to start your second line agent in that second five minute window.

00:50:41.420 --> 00:50:43.180
Speaker 1: So between five and ten minutes.

00:50:43.500 --> 00:50:45.420
Speaker 1: I recall that in the

00:50:45.940 --> 00:50:53.540
Speaker 1: First EM cases algorithm we had on this from episode 133, that window was a bit later, but now we're suggesting to do it earlier.

00:50:54.160 --> 00:51:01.920
Speaker 1: The second line medication of choice is levaturacetem or kepra, and that's going to be up to four and a half grams.

00:51:02.000 --> 00:51:05.680
Speaker 1: And the key thing there is that it's also often underdosed.

00:51:05.940 --> 00:51:10.660
Speaker 1: So we're giving up to four and a half grams of levitaracetam.

00:51:10.980 --> 00:51:19.619
Speaker 1: If you choose to give ketamine, you can give it early, like with your second benzodose at one milligram per kilogram.

00:51:19.859 --> 00:51:25.220
Speaker 1: Sarah's recommendation is to use it a bit later as your third or fourth line medication.

00:51:25.540 --> 00:51:31.780
Speaker 1: Propofol is probably your go-to induction agent for refractory status.

00:51:32.120 --> 00:51:36.040
Speaker 1: Make sure you use a high dose when you start your infusion.

00:51:36.200 --> 00:51:40.200
Speaker 1: So we're talking 80 to 100 micrograms per kilogram per minute.

00:51:40.860 --> 00:51:49.260
Speaker 1: And that you will almost certainly need to have norepinephrine on board because these patients pretty much universally will dump their blood pressure.

00:51:49.420 --> 00:51:50.940
Speaker 1: Don't skimp on the propofol.

00:51:51.680 --> 00:51:59.599
Speaker 1: And at this point, I'm hoping that you've already called your intensivist because then you know you've got more difficult decisions to make.

00:51:59.680 --> 00:52:02.000
Speaker 1: You know, does this patient now go on leucosamide?

00:52:02.200 --> 00:52:04.760
Speaker 1: If you haven't given ketamine, is this when you give ketamine?

00:52:05.000 --> 00:52:14.200
Speaker 1: We didn't even talk about phenobarbital, which is another another option, which has become more popular for severe alcohol withdrawal.

00:52:14.359 --> 00:52:23.240
Speaker 1: If you have a patient with known withdrawal seizures from alcohol, that phenobarbitol might be a great third or fourth line choice.

00:52:23.559 --> 00:52:25.319
Speaker 1: There's inhaled anesthetics.

00:52:25.480 --> 00:52:26.760
Speaker 1: There's all kinds of other stuff.

00:52:26.920 --> 00:52:31.960
Speaker 1: But I think for me as an emergency provider, now we're in like you were like 20 minutes in.

00:52:32.260 --> 00:52:34.180
Speaker 1: And now I'm getting the intensivist on the call.

00:52:34.260 --> 00:52:37.940
Speaker 1: I've done pretty much everything I can, and there's a lot of options there.

00:52:38.100 --> 00:52:40.100
Speaker 1: And I think by this time my brain is probably

00:52:40.640 --> 00:52:43.920
Speaker 1: completely full and I have no other options to give the patients.

00:52:44.000 --> 00:52:51.359
Speaker 3: So Well, and you're moving well out of, you know, the space where we have good evidence and into expert opinion.

00:52:51.300 --> 00:52:53.300
Speaker 3: Uh you've intubated the patient by then.

00:52:53.540 --> 00:52:54.020
Speaker 3: Absolutely.

00:52:54.100 --> 00:53:00.260
Speaker 3: I'm getting my intensivist on board or my neurologist because often they're the gatekeeper to the EEG.

00:53:00.500 --> 00:53:02.980
Speaker 3: So definitely a good time to be making those phone calls.

00:53:03.240 --> 00:53:03.800
Speaker 1: Great.

00:53:04.040 --> 00:53:10.360
Speaker 1: And um I understand you're no longer practicing in the ICU, uh, but you were for how many years was it about 15 years?

00:53:10.440 --> 00:53:11.480
Speaker 1: You were in 20.

00:53:11.720 --> 00:53:13.240
Speaker 1: 20 years as an intensivist.

00:53:13.640 --> 00:53:14.680
Speaker 2: As an intensivist.

00:53:15.539 --> 00:53:20.819
Speaker 1: So to sort of wrap up here, let's say I've done all of this.

00:53:20.980 --> 00:53:23.619
Speaker 1: I've given a few doses of benzos.

00:53:23.700 --> 00:53:27.059
Speaker 1: I I've intubated the patient with propofol and sucks.

00:53:27.460 --> 00:53:37.700
Speaker 1: I've given my second line medication, uh, levitaracetem, uh, I've started them on a propofol drip at a high dose, they're still seizing, or maybe they've they've settled.

00:53:37.860 --> 00:53:39.700
Speaker 1: When you get that phone call

00:53:39.960 --> 00:53:45.640
Speaker 1: What do you usually recommend in your run-of-the-mill refractory status epilepticus?

00:53:45.720 --> 00:53:49.720
Speaker 1: Or I think there's another name for once they're refractory to your refractory treatments.

00:53:50.120 --> 00:53:51.880
Speaker 1: I can't remember what it is, but it's like super refractory.

00:53:52.920 --> 00:53:53.000
Speaker 1: Yeah.

00:53:53.240 --> 00:53:57.480
Speaker 1: Once you have a super refractory patient, what are your usual recommendations?

00:53:57.800 --> 00:53:58.040
Speaker 3: Yeah.

00:54:01.120 --> 00:54:09.120
Speaker 3: Uh so typically these patients who remain refractory will be on max doses of propofol by an infusion.

00:54:09.260 --> 00:54:13.580
Speaker 3: They'll typically on be on max doses of a benzodiazepine by infusion.

00:54:13.740 --> 00:54:18.700
Speaker 3: We'll often start ketamine first boluses and then also an infusion of that.

00:54:18.859 --> 00:54:26.540
Speaker 3: They will be on long-standing high-dose anti-seizure maintenance medications like Levatyra cetem, Valproic, et cetera.

00:54:26.700 --> 00:54:32.460
Speaker 3: And we'll continue to add those until we can get them to stop seizing on EEG.

00:54:33.220 --> 00:54:42.900
Speaker 3: Once they've stopped seizing on EEG, they're on all of these medications, then the job is to start gradually weaning them off.

00:54:43.140 --> 00:54:47.700
Speaker 3: without the seizure recurring, so we can eventually get them extibated.

00:54:47.779 --> 00:54:50.500
Speaker 3: But that's a process that tends to take days and days and days.

00:54:50.900 --> 00:54:51.140
Speaker 1: All right.

00:54:51.220 --> 00:54:54.579
Speaker 1: Yeah, great to know what actually happens in the ICU with these patients.

00:54:55.020 --> 00:55:00.859
Speaker 1: One thing we didn't even touch on is non-convulsive status epilepticus.

00:55:01.420 --> 00:55:03.500
Speaker 1: And we're not going to get deep into this, but

00:55:03.740 --> 00:55:06.140
Speaker 1: Just a few tricks, pearls.

00:55:06.220 --> 00:55:09.260
Speaker 1: I mean I was speaking with Katie Lynn recently.

00:55:09.660 --> 00:55:10.860
Speaker 1: Her tip was just uh

00:55:11.440 --> 00:55:19.119
Speaker 1: just take a good long look at the eyes 'cause most of these patients who don't appear to be seizing in their limbs

00:55:19.240 --> 00:55:23.480
Speaker 1: There's almost always a clue in their eyes that they're still seizing.

00:55:23.640 --> 00:55:27.720
Speaker 1: So what we call non-convulsive status epilepticus is actually

00:55:28.040 --> 00:55:31.640
Speaker 1: you know, if you really pay attention to those eyes, you can see that they're still seizing.

00:55:31.800 --> 00:55:37.080
Speaker 1: Any other just tips or practical stuff you can tell us about non-convulsive status?

00:55:37.480 --> 00:55:39.720
Speaker 3: Yeah, so it's a great question, Anton.

00:55:39.800 --> 00:55:41.880
Speaker 3: I've seen this a bunch of times.

00:55:42.020 --> 00:55:49.140
Speaker 3: And almost every time the patient to me just starts by looking a bit odd.

00:55:49.540 --> 00:55:58.420
Speaker 3: Like they're staring or blinking or not responding appropriately or doing a little bit of unusual twitching

00:55:58.640 --> 00:56:05.920
Speaker 3: And I guess I now just have a mental flag that when I see that, I need to ask myself, is this patient seizing?

00:56:06.000 --> 00:56:07.839
Speaker 3: Like is this non-convulsive status?

00:56:08.140 --> 00:56:12.700
Speaker 3: And I get really serious about the physical exam, completely uncovering them.

00:56:12.859 --> 00:56:17.180
Speaker 3: I want to see all the limbs without any sheets, clothes, etc.

00:56:17.300 --> 00:56:20.740
Speaker 3: I want to have a close look at the eyes, the way Katie does for sure.

00:56:20.980 --> 00:56:27.620
Speaker 3: And if I'm not sure, I default to treating them as if they're seizing

00:56:27.660 --> 00:56:32.620
Speaker 3: Because non-convulsive status is just as bad for your brain as convulsive status.

00:56:32.780 --> 00:56:37.980
Speaker 3: You're still burning neurons at a very high rate, and outcomes remain miserable.

00:56:38.340 --> 00:56:43.540
Speaker 3: So if I'm not sure I err on the side of caution and treat them like status epilepticus.

00:56:43.860 --> 00:56:44.020
Speaker 1: Right.

00:56:44.180 --> 00:56:48.100
Speaker 1: And then you know you got the diagnosis if you give them your first dose of benzo.

00:56:48.359 --> 00:56:51.400
Speaker 1: And they suddenly start talking to you and they seem perfectly normal then.

00:56:51.720 --> 00:56:53.160
Speaker 3: Yes, which I have had.

00:56:53.240 --> 00:56:54.680
Speaker 3: And you feel like a hero.

00:56:54.839 --> 00:56:59.079
Speaker 3: You give them four of Larazipam and they blink and they look at you and they're like, oh, what was that?

00:56:59.240 --> 00:57:00.440
Speaker 3: That was weird.

00:57:00.440 --> 00:57:03.720
Speaker 3: those moments in emergency medicine continue to blow my mind.

00:57:04.920 --> 00:57:05.640
Speaker 1: All right, great.

00:57:05.720 --> 00:57:07.240
Speaker 1: Well thanks so much, Sarah.

00:57:07.480 --> 00:57:09.400
Speaker 1: That was a really fun discussion.

00:57:09.640 --> 00:57:10.839
Speaker 1: Yeah, thanks for having me.

